/**
 * `serve` CLI verb - launches `boxlang-miniserver` (a separate standalone
 * binary, confirmed via ortus-boxlang/boxlang-miniserver source; this module
 * never boots a server in-process) on a built project's `.build/public`, via
 * `.build/miniserver.json`.
 *
 * Requires a prior `build`. If `boxlang-miniserver` isn't found on PATH,
 * fails with a clear, actionable error rather than silently doing nothing.
 *
 * ### What is served
 *
 * `.build` follows the ColdBox BoxLang template (see models/build/layout/BuildLayout): the code is `app/`, the
 * web root is `public/`, and nothing else is reachable over HTTP. `miniserver.json` names every folder by a
 * path relative to `.build`, and the server is started with `.build` as its working directory, which is
 * what the template's `${user-dir}` means. The same file therefore serves a project from where it was built, a
 * packaged copy unpacked anywhere, and a container.
 *
 * ### Which modules the served app loads
 *
 * Exactly the `bx-agents` module (internal alias `bxagents`, with the modules nested in it) and the project's own `boxlang_modules/`:
 * the module is copied into `.build/runtime/modules`, under its installed folder name (see ModuleCopier), and `serverHome` is
 * `.build/runtime`, so BoxLang's `<home>/modules` is that folder. This verb used to add the machine's own
 * module directory to the served app's config, which loaded every module installed there into the served app.
 * It no longer names that directory anywhere. Measured: with neither `serverHome` nor BOXLANG_HOME set, BoxLang
 * uses `~/.boxlang/modules`, and a decoy module placed there loaded; with the home set to `.build/runtime`
 * the module list was exactly bx-agents (alias bxagents) and its nested modules.
 *
 * ### Engine config
 *
 * `configPath` is the template's `runtime/boxlang.json` (with its whitespace compression turned off, see
 * `templates/coldbox-boxlang/VERSION`). A `config/boxlang.json` under the home is ignored when `configPath` is
 * given (measured with a decoy), so this verb no longer writes one.
 *
 * ### Why whitespace compression is off
 *
 * The web runtime's whitespace compression is on by default and collapses blank lines, and a Server-Sent
 * Events frame ends with one. Measured on BoxLang and boxlang-miniserver 1.19.0, with it ON and OFF:
 *
 *   - `POST /__bxagents/stream` (a native `SSE()` emitter through ColdBox `toAi()`): 14 frame terminators, 14 frames,
 *     8 content deltas both ways; `route().toSSE()` 5 and a two-line `SSE()` page 4, intact both ways. The native
 *     emitter's output evidently does not pass through the filter.
 *   - a handler that writes an event stream by hand with `writeOutput( "event: tick\ndata: hello N\n\n" )` three
 *     times: 3 terminators with compression off, 0 with it on (77 bytes against 82).
 *
 * So on 1.19.0 the setting only matters for hand-written event streams, and an earlier note here claiming the
 * native stream lost its terminators did not reproduce. It stays off in the vendored runtime/boxlang.json as a safety net: it costs
 * nothing and keeps a hand-written stream (and any older engine that did filter the emitter) working.
 *
 * ### Binding
 *
 * Binds to `127.0.0.1` (loopback) unless `--host` says otherwise. It used to
 * default to `0.0.0.0`, which put the served agent in reach of everyone on
 * the network by default - and the generated web chat UI's API-key gate is
 * OPT-IN (only generated when a gateway entry declares `apiKeyEnvVar`), so
 * that default meant an unauthenticated agent, driving the developer's own
 * provider API key, on every interface. `--host=0.0.0.0` still exposes it
 * that way when that is genuinely what's wanted, and the verb's own output
 * always states which interface it bound to.
 *
 * This reaches `serve` and anything that reuses it (`invoke --server`, `chat --server`) - they spawn a brand-new JVM
 * whose launch config this verb writes. `chat` without `--server` also starts a child BoxLang CLI process, but
 * supplies the project's `runtime/boxlang.json` and `runtime/` home directly. The remaining in-process verbs
 * (`build`, `test`, default `invoke`) use the ALREADY-STARTED `bxAgents` process, whose own BoxLang runtime (and
 * therefore its home) was resolved before any of our BoxLang code ever got a chance to run. `BoxRuntime` is a
 * JVM-wide singleton; its home is fixed at first initialization.
 */
class {

	/**
	 * What this verb is: read by the dispatcher, the help, the completions and the reference page.
	 */
	struct function meta() {
		return {
			name : "serve",
			group : "BUILD & RUN",
			position : 4,
			description : "Start the HTTP server",
			usage : "[options]",
			options : [
				{ name : "port", display : "--port=<number>", description : "Listening port, 1-65535. Default: 8080.", takesValue : true },
				{ name : "host", display : "--host=<address>", description : "Bind address. Default: 127.0.0.1.", takesValue : true }
			],
			examples : [ "serve", "serve --port=9000" ],
			note : "Requires a prior build and boxlang-miniserver on PATH. Use --host=0.0.0.0 deliberately to expose the server beyond this machine."
		}
	}


	/**
	 * Runs the serve verb, spawning a new server process with the specified options.
	 *
	 * Returns:
	 *   - buildDir: the absolute path to the generated `.build` directory
	 *   - configPath: the absolute path to the generated `.build/miniserver.json`
	 *   - database: the absolute path to the project's chat database
	 *   - exitCode: 0 when the server process starts successfully
	 *   - host: the interface the server binds to
	 *   - message: the served URL, app directory and binding address, plus any exposure warning
	 *   - port: the resolved integer port the server listens on
	 *   - process: the spawned server process, for the caller to manage its lifecycle
	 *   - publicDir: the absolute path to the served web root (`.build/public`)
	 *   - serverHome: the BoxLang home directory for this project (`.build/runtime`)
	 *   - warning: a warning when the server is exposed beyond loopback; otherwise blank
	 *
	 * @options { projectRoot (required, absolute path), port (optional, defaults to 8080 - must be a whole number 1-65535), host (optional, defaults to 127.0.0.1 - pass 0.0.0.0 to expose the agent on every network interface) }
	 */
	struct function run( struct options = {} ) {
		var projectRoot = trim( arguments.options.projectRoot ?: "" )
		if ( !len( projectRoot ) ) {
			throw( type = "BxAgents.MissingProjectRoot", message = "`serve` requires a project root" )
		}

		var root = projectRoot.reReplace( "[\/]+$", "" )
		var layout = new "models.build.layout.BuildLayout@bxagents"().of( root )
		var appDir = layout.appDir

		if ( !directoryExists( appDir ) ) {
			throw(
				type    = "BxAgents.NotBuilt",
				message = "No build found at [#appDir#] - run `build` first"
			)
		}
		if ( !directoryExists( layout.publicDir ) ) {
			throw(
				type    = "BxAgents.NotBuilt",
				message = "The build at [#layout.buildDir#] has no public/ folder: it was made before the app/public layout. Run `build` again."
			)
		}

		// `_path` replaces the machine's PATH as the folders to search, for tests: a machine that has the miniserver installed, such as a
		// CI runner, could never show the "not found" answer otherwise
		var launcher = createObject( "java", "ortus.boxlang.bxagents.build.MiniServerLauncher" )
		var executable = structKeyExists( arguments.options, "_path" ) ? launcher.findExecutable( arguments.options._path ) : launcher.findExecutable()
		if ( isNull( executable ) ) {
			throw(
				type    = "BxAgents.MiniServerNotFound",
				message = "boxlang-miniserver was not found on PATH. Install it (see https://boxlang.ortusbooks.com/getting-started/running-boxlang/miniserver) and try again."
			)
		}

		var port = resolvePort( arguments.options.port ?: 8080 )
		// Loopback by DEFAULT - see the class docblock. `--host=0.0.0.0`
		// still opts into every interface, deliberately and visibly.
		var host = arguments.options.host ?: "127.0.0.1"
		var boundTo = host == "0.0.0.0" ? "ALL network interfaces" : "#host# only"
		var serverHome = layout.runtimeDir

		// The served app needs the bxagents module in its own runtime (and nothing else from the machine). The build put it
		// there; this makes sure of it, which is a no-op while its stamp is unchanged.
		var copied = new "models.build.layout.ModuleCopier@bxagents"().copy( new "models.build.layout.ModuleCopier@bxagents"().sourceDir( arguments.options.moduleDir ?: "" ), serverHome )
		// the copy keeps the installed folder's name (`bx-agents` from `bx-module install`), so any folder there is the module
		var installedCopies = directoryExists( serverHome & "/modules" ) ? directoryList( path = serverHome & "/modules", recurse = false, type = "dir" ) : []
		if ( !installedCopies.len() ) {
			throw(
				type    = "BxAgents.NoModuleInBuild",
				message = "The build has no bxagents module in [#serverHome#/modules], so the served app could not load. It is copied from the installed module: run `build` with BX Agents installed as a module, or set BXAGENTS_MODULE_DIR to the built module folder."
			)
		}

		var configPath = new "models.build.layout.MiniServerJson@bxagents"().write( layout.buildDir, port, host )

		// The web chat and `chat` open the same database file, in the project's data/ folder
		var paths = new "models.store.ChatStorePath@bxagents"()
		var databaseFile = paths.prepared( root )
		var environment = createObject( "java", "java.util.HashMap" ).init()
		environment.put( javaCast( "string", paths.variableName() ), javaCast( "string", databaseFile ) )
		// This project's own runtime home, also through the child's environment: a BOXLANG_HOME already set in the
		// environment this process inherited would otherwise be the home of every project served from here.
		environment.put( "BOXLANG_HOME", javaCast( "string", serverHome ) )
		// `_environment` adds variables to the child's, for a test that needs the server to see a different machine (see ServedLayoutSpec)
		for ( var extra in ( arguments.options._environment ?: {} ) ) {
			environment.put( javaCast( "string", extra ), javaCast( "string", arguments.options._environment[ extra ] ) )
		}

		// started in .build: the paths in miniserver.json are relative to it, and so is ${user-dir} in the engine config
		var process = createObject( "java", "ortus.boxlang.bxagents.build.MiniServerLauncher" ).launch( executable, configPath, true, environment, layout.buildDir )

		// an open API that other machines can reach is said out loud, once, at the start
		var warning = new "models.api.ExposureWarning@bxagents"().of( appDir, host, port )

		return {
			exitCode   : 0,
			message    : "Serving [#layout.publicDir#] (the app is in [#appDir#]) on http://#host#:#port# - bound to #boundTo#" & ( len( warning ) ? char( 10 ) & warning : "" ),
			warning    : warning,
			process    : process,
			configPath : configPath,
			host       : host,
			port       : port,
			serverHome : serverHome,
			buildDir   : layout.buildDir,
			publicDir  : layout.publicDir,
			database   : databaseFile
		}
	}

	/**
	 * `--port=8080` arrives from argv as the STRING "8080", while this verb's
	 * own default is the NUMBER 8080 - so `.build/miniserver.json` came out
	 * with a different shape (`"port": "8080"` vs `"port": 8080`) depending
	 * on whether the flag was passed at all, and a nonsense `--port=abc` was
	 * only ever caught inside the launched miniserver child process, long
	 * after this verb reported success. Validated and cast to a real integer
	 * here instead, so the generated config always has one shape and a bad
	 * port fails immediately, here, with a message about `--port`.
	 *
	 * @port The raw `port` option - a string from argv, or a number from a direct caller (e.g. `invoke --server`)
	 *
	 * @return The port as an integer
	 */
	private numeric function resolvePort( required any port ) {
		var candidate = trim( toString( arguments.port ) )

		if ( !reFind( "^[0-9]+$", candidate ) || val( candidate ) < 1 || val( candidate ) > 65535 ) {
			throw(
				type    = "BxAgents.InvalidPort",
				message = "Invalid --port [#candidate#] - must be a whole number between 1 and 65535"
			)
		}

		return javaCast( "int", val( candidate ) )
	}

}
